--
[over.ics.user]/1 with [over.best.ics]/4: a user-defined conversion sequence
contains at most one user-defined conversion; the argument/this conversions of
a candidate constructor or conversion operator are standard conversion
sequences and may not themselves require a user-defined conversion.

KNOWNBUG: when resolving the construction of MS from an MD (a user-defined
conversion), the front end realises a candidate's argument conversions by
constructing temporaries and re-enters overload resolution without enforcing the
"at most one user-defined conversion" rule.  For mutually-convertible class
types -- each constructible from the other -- this recurses without bound
(MS's copy/move constructor needs MD -> MS, which needs MD -> MS, ...),
exhausting memory and crashing (SIGSEGV).

Minimal model of libstdc++'s std::ranges::__detail::__max_size_type /
__max_diff_type, reached when __to_unsigned_like constructs __max_size_type from
__max_diff_type.  Reclassify CORE once the construction/overload-resolution path
restricts candidate argument conversions to standard conversion sequences.
